{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: when the last 10–20 days show a strong uptrend, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model underestimates by ~+2 to +6 €/t, often largest around days 5–20).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: if momentum is positive (recent returns > 0 and higher highs), steepen the near-term slope (days 1–10) because the model ramps too slowly into rallies.",
    "Hump-shaped error profile: apply the biggest uplift in the mid-horizon (roughly days 7–18), tapering toward the end of the 30-day horizon where bias is smaller and can flip slightly negative.",
    "Over-extended late-horizon after peaks: when spot has recently peaked and started to roll over, reduce the far-horizon tail (days 20–30) because the model tends to stay too high and misses the subsequent drawdown.",
    "Mean-reversion timing mismatch: after sharp run-ups, expect earlier and deeper pullbacks than forecast; bring forward the turning point by ~3–7 days and increase the drawdown magnitude modestly."
  ]
}
